  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nDome of high pressure to our west will remain an influence as it\ncontinues to bleed into the area. Post-frontal northwesterly\nflow allowing for cooler temperatures and a drier, more \ncomfortable airmass to filter in. Patchy valley fog is possible\nthis morning, but elevated flow aloft and streams of cloud cover\nwill likely interrupt a widespread process from manifesting.\nHigher likelihood exists across the northern mountain valleys of\nWest Virginia where fog may be locally dense.\n\nWhile the area will be mostly dry today, a quick moving\ndisturbance may interrupt our high pressure scheme and bring \nisolated afternoon showers and thunderstorms to the area. 15-25%\nchances exist across our Ohio and northern West Virginia counties\nfor the afternoon with these chances shifting to the mountains\ntonight. Sunday will be dry ahead of our next potent system that\nwill arrive Sunday night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nThe arrival of the next round of heavy rainfall and\nthunderstorms remains disputed. At some point Sunday\nevening/Sunday night, a disturbance moves in from the west\nbringing rounds of showers and thunderstorms Sunday night\nthrough Monday. Models agree that the Mid-Ohio River Valley,\nincluding all of our Ohio will receive the first drops from this\nsystem. They however vary with onset timing, but do agree that\nit will likely be between 10PM Sunday and 1AM Monday. The NAM\nand GFS models side with the earlier end of that range.\n\nPrecipitable water is modeled to be between 1.50\" and 2.00\" \nwhich will make for efficient rainfall rates that could cause \nlocalized to isolated flash flooding. WPC has a slight risk for \nexcessive rainfall (level 2/4) across our Ohio counties Sunday \nnight and then a bulk of the area on Monday. Latest WPC QPF has \nbetween 1.00\" and 2.00\" of total rainfall across our area from \nSunday night into Monday night with the heaviest falling Monday\nmorning. The higher amounts are projected to be across our Ohio\ncounties and northern central lowland counties of West Virginia.\n\nThere is the possibility a flood watch for flash flooding will\nbe needed, especially if amounts trend upwards. The Canadian and\nNAM models all show locally higher amounts being picked up\nsomewhere in the Mid-Ohio Valley and central lowlands. \n\nThere will be a conditional risk for strong to severe \nthunderstorms with this event, mostly across our southern \nmountain and coalfield counties of West Virginia and Virginia. \nClouds and moisture will likely keep convective activity from \nbeing numerous, but latest NAM guidance does show upwards of \n2,000-2,500 J/kg of CAPE across southwestern VA and southern WV \nMonday afternoon, right as a cold front starts to approach. It \nis possible that some thunderstorms could be on the strong to \nsevere side with damaging winds being the main concern.",
